.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 1129 AM PDT Sat Oct 31 2026
(This afternoon through Sunday)

Chances for rain showers remain greatest (20-50%) across the
mountains of San Bentio County and interior portions of Monterey
County through the rest of the day today. This is all head of a
cold front that will approach from the northwest and move
southeastward across the North Bay late this evening (between 7 -
11 PM PDT), across the rest of the Bay Area (between 11 PM PDT - 6
AM Sunday PST), and across the Central Coast (between 1 - 7 AM
Sunday PST). As the front moves through the region overnight,
there is a 10-20% probability of thunderstorms, largely over the
coastal waters. Post frontal rain showers may linger offshore and
along the Monterey County coastline through early Sunday
afternoon. All in all, rainfall amounts are forecast to be on the
light side through Sunday afternoon with the greatest probability
of measuring >0.25" being in the coastal ranges and lesser amounts
as you move farther inland.

By Sunday afternoon, expect mostly dry and clearing sky
conditions as weak ridging builds in from the eastern Pacific in
wake of the exiting trough. Temperatures will remain seasonably
cool with afternoon highs in the 60s and overnight lows in the 40s
to lower 50s with the coldest regions of the North Bay
potentially reaching into the upper 30s both tonight and Sunday
night. Again, coastal flooding remains possible along the bayshore
locations of the San Francisco, San Pablo, and Monterey Bays
through Sunday at 6 PM PST with the Pacific Coast dropping off at
6 PM PDT this evening. For more information on that please see the
CFWMTR product.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 1129 AM PDT Sat Oct 31 2026
(Sunday night through next Friday)

The next chance for rain across the region will be from early
Tuesday morning through Wednesday morning as an upper level cut-
off low approaches the northern California coast from the west.
But again, not expecting widespread heavy rainfall from this
system. The placement of the cut-off low remains in question from
Monday night through Wednesday. A farther north track would result
in less rainfall across the Bay Area and Central Coast vs. a more
southern track. Dry weather now looks to return for the remainder
of next week with a gradual warming trend heading into next
weekend.

